WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:04.587
You're called to create a post-apocalyptic 
giraffe astronaut.

00:00:04.587 --> 00:00:05.797
Generated.

00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:09.259
Genghis Khan playing a guitar solo,
pixel art.

00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:10.677
Generated.

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:13.513
A man holding a 
delicious apple...

00:00:15.432 --> 00:00:16.933
What's with his hands?

00:00:19.394 --> 00:00:22.313
Why can't AI art 
make hands?

00:00:22.731 --> 00:00:25.400
It doesn't matter what
AI art model you use.

00:00:25.442 --> 00:00:28.028
If you have a man holding 
a delicious apple

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:30.280
his hands will look weird 
holding it.

00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:32.657
Why is this so hard?

00:00:32.657 --> 00:00:33.950
Seems easy enough, right?

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.996
We've got this weird situation 
where AI art instantly make...

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:41.791
Abraham Lincoln dressed like 
glam David Bowie.

00:00:41.791 --> 00:00:45.336
But struggles with a woman 
holding a cell phone.

00:00:46.588 --> 00:00:48.381
This isn't just a weird glitch.

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:50.800
The struggle of AI art 
with hands

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.053
can actually teach you 
something bigger...

00:00:53.344 --> 00:00:55.930
about how AI art works.

00:01:02.645 --> 00:01:05.774
I mean, what is so hard
about this?

00:01:09.694 --> 00:01:12.489
I asked an artist who has taught 
thousands of people...

00:01:12.697 --> 00:01:14.991
how to draw hands 
from imagination.

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:17.118
Before someone becomes

00:01:17.118 --> 00:01:20.371
or starts training 
to be an artist.

00:01:20.371 --> 00:01:21.664
Like officially training.

00:01:21.664 --> 00:01:22.999
It's pattern recognition.

00:01:22.999 --> 00:01:25.168
You just grow up seeing 
a whole bunch of hands...

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.962
and you start knowing 
what hands look like.

00:01:27.962 --> 00:01:30.381
You learn how things look 
by living in the world

00:01:30.381 --> 00:01:32.509
and recognizing patterns.

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:36.554
An AI is similar but has 
key differences.

00:01:36.971 --> 00:01:39.057
Imagine an AI is like you...

00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:41.476
but trapped in a museum 
from birth.

00:01:42.185 --> 00:01:45.021
All the machine has to learn from 
are the pictures...

00:01:45.313 --> 00:01:47.774
and the little placards 
on the side.

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:51.277
Apple: A red apple on a 
brown table.

00:01:51.986 --> 00:01:54.405
That's like the images 
it sees from the web

00:01:54.405 --> 00:01:56.407
and the descriptions 
that go with them.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:00.453
It's similar to how you learn, 
but locked in that museum.

00:02:00.703 --> 00:02:02.622
If you want to understand 
an apple

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.833
you can rotate it 
in your hand.

00:02:04.874 --> 00:02:06.918
You can watch it 
whenever you want.

00:02:07.335 --> 00:02:09.879
If AI wants to understand
an apple

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:13.383
it has to find another picture of 
an apple in the museum.

00:02:13.675 --> 00:02:16.886
Pattern recognition has allowed
AI and people

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:18.680
to draw decent apples...

00:02:19.389 --> 00:02:21.224
but the processes differ.

00:02:21.516 --> 00:02:24.102
You start training to become an artist,
and now you're like

00:02:24.102 --> 00:02:26.688
okay, now I have to learn 
the rules.

00:02:26.688 --> 00:02:29.816
And that's where it becomes very different 
from how AI is learning.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.486
Artists, in order to draw
something complicated

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:37.991
we tend to simplify things
into basic forms.

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:40.034
And so when you 
look at a hand...

00:02:40.243 --> 00:02:44.497
you pretty much have 
the big blocky part of the palm, right?

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:46.457
You have the front,
you have the back

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:47.917
and then you have 
the thickness.

00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:49.419
So you can pretty much 
just make that

00:02:49.419 --> 00:02:52.172
into like a square with some 
thickness to it.

00:02:52.213 --> 00:02:54.424
Then an artist can add
all the style 

00:02:54.424 --> 00:02:57.802
and texture and detail
they want.

00:02:57.802 --> 00:02:59.387
AI works differently.

00:02:59.387 --> 00:03:00.555
Look at this hand.

00:03:00.555 --> 00:03:03.349
The shapes are bizarre, 
but the AI has done

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:06.102
a great job showing the light 
and texture here.

00:03:07.061 --> 00:03:10.106
Remember, the AI knows 
how things look

00:03:10.106 --> 00:03:11.316
but not how they work.

00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:14.944
So these patterns in pixels 
are easy to understand.

00:03:15.612 --> 00:03:17.238
It never learned, 
however

00:03:17.238 --> 00:03:20.241
that fingers don't really 
bend like this.

00:03:20.283 --> 00:03:22.035
It doesn't simplify the forms.

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:23.995
Remember, it's trapped 
in the museum

00:03:23.995 --> 00:03:27.999
so it is just trying to guess
where hand-like pixels should be.

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:31.002
Without knowing how hands
work like we do.

00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:34.422
But listen, I find this 
kind of dissatisfying.

00:03:34.422 --> 00:03:37.175
I mean, I'm basically just saying 
that AI can't draw hands

00:03:37.175 --> 00:03:38.551
because it's not a person.

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:41.721
But AI also doesn't know 
anything about construction

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:44.682
and it can still make 
a beautiful skyscraper

00:03:44.682 --> 00:03:46.267
in New York City.

00:03:48.144 --> 00:03:49.520
So to understand this better

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.064
I spoke to two people 
who have worked

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:52.690
with generative art models.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:56.527
Yilun Du is a grad student whose 
heart is in robotics.

00:03:56.527 --> 00:03:59.197
But, you know, AI art is 
like a big deal now.

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:00.823
So, he got pulled into it.

00:04:00.823 --> 00:04:02.617
Because of how popular
these models have been 

00:04:02.617 --> 00:04:03.660
in generative art....

00:04:04.035 --> 00:04:05.828
I've also been working
on that.

00:04:05.828 --> 00:04:07.163
And I talked to Roy Shilkrot

00:04:07.163 --> 00:04:08.957
who has a super varied resume

00:04:08.957 --> 00:04:12.543
but has been teaching about 
generative art since 2018.

00:04:12.627 --> 00:04:14.629
Good students 
that come in....

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:16.881
that are trying to break
those models

00:04:16.881 --> 00:04:17.924
take them to the next level.

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:19.467
Talking to them helped
 me figure out

00:04:19.467 --> 00:04:20.677
three big reasons.

00:04:20.677 --> 00:04:23.388
Not every reason, but 
three big reasons

00:04:23.388 --> 00:04:26.432
that hands are tough 
for AI art models.

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:28.851
The data size and quality

00:04:28.851 --> 00:04:32.605
the way hands act 
and the low margin for error.

00:04:33.064 --> 00:04:36.526
For the data size, let's go back 
to the museum idea.

00:04:36.526 --> 00:04:38.403
The museum the robot 
hangs out in

00:04:38.403 --> 00:04:41.322
it has a ton of rooms 
dedicated to faces...

00:04:41.823 --> 00:04:43.992
but not so many rooms 
for hands.

00:04:43.992 --> 00:04:46.077
That means it has less 
to learn from.

00:04:46.077 --> 00:04:48.121
Just as an example, 
available datasets

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:51.249
like Flickr HQ has 
70,000 faces.

00:04:51.499 --> 00:04:53.376
70,000.

00:04:53.376 --> 00:04:57.922
And this popular one annotates 
200,000 pics of celebrity faces...

00:04:58.172 --> 00:05:02.176
for lots of details like eyeglasses 
or pointy noses.

00:05:03.011 --> 00:05:05.263
There are a ton of great 
hand datasets

00:05:05.263 --> 00:05:07.265
that can really understand hands

00:05:07.265 --> 00:05:09.475
like this one with 11,000 hands.

00:05:09.976 --> 00:05:14.856
But these may not have been used 
to train the AI that makes art.

00:05:15.273 --> 00:05:17.942
That data scarcity combines 
with the quality

00:05:17.942 --> 00:05:20.153
and complexity of the data.

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:22.322
Hands data in the art museum

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:24.824
isn't yet annotated to show 
how they work.

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:27.285
Like the celebrities pointy noses.

00:05:27.285 --> 00:05:28.619
What they say is...

00:05:28.786 --> 00:05:31.289
there is an image and there is 
a person in the image

00:05:31.289 --> 00:05:33.374
and that person is holding 
an umbrella.

00:05:33.374 --> 00:05:35.376
You don't give the machine 
a lot of clues

00:05:35.376 --> 00:05:37.628
saying this is a person holding t
he umbrella.

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:40.798
The thumb is going 
from one side

00:05:40.798 --> 00:05:43.843
of the handle and the fingers 
are curled...

00:05:43.885 --> 00:05:46.596
and then thumb is covering
the index finger

00:05:46.596 --> 00:05:47.722
but not the other one.

00:05:49.557 --> 00:05:52.810
All that is made worse because 
hands do lots of things

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.562
compared to, 
say... faces.

00:05:54.562 --> 00:05:57.815
So there's a pretty common like 
portrait photo face.

00:05:57.815 --> 00:05:59.400
There are a lot of these 
photos online

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:00.985
and the thing is everything is 
very well centered, right?

00:06:00.985 --> 00:06:02.445
Like eyes are always 
around here.

00:06:02.445 --> 00:06:04.197
Like there's always this order.

00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:07.367
That's not true of hands 
which can do this

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.577
and this and this.

00:06:09.952 --> 00:06:12.038
I swear I'm sober right now.

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:13.122
Stan mentioned this, too.

00:06:13.122 --> 00:06:15.083
How many fingers do you see right now?

00:06:15.083 --> 00:06:17.377
Like two or three.

00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:18.503
Like it doesn't know 
there's five.

00:06:18.503 --> 00:06:19.629
Because sometimes there's two

00:06:19.629 --> 00:06:20.546
sometimes there's three

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:22.048
sometimes four, sometimes five.

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:24.759
You can see these problems 
with AI hands

00:06:24.759 --> 00:06:28.054
but the jankiness 
is all over AI art.

00:06:28.304 --> 00:06:29.347
Just look at horses.

00:06:29.347 --> 00:06:31.808
You can also have like three legs, 
five legs, six legs.

00:06:32.016 --> 00:06:34.477
The model does not learn to explain this
because there's too much diversity

00:06:34.477 --> 00:06:36.646
and it doesn't have as much bias as we do.

00:06:36.938 --> 00:06:38.648
Okay. Did you hear that 
last part he said?

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:41.984
Good, because it's 
really important.

00:06:41.984 --> 00:06:43.986
It doesn't have as much bias 
as we do.

00:06:43.986 --> 00:06:47.782
We care a lot about hands and 
need them to be perfect.

00:06:48.074 --> 00:06:50.284
There is a low margin for error.

00:06:50.827 --> 00:06:53.538
But because the model doesn't 
understand hands 

00:06:53.538 --> 00:06:56.791
hasn't seen many and 
because hands act weird...

00:06:57.125 --> 00:07:01.170
it makes pictures that are like 
hands it’s seen in the museum

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:03.256
but not an exact hand.

00:07:03.256 --> 00:07:06.426
That's good enough for a ton of stuff, 
but not hands.

00:07:06.551 --> 00:07:07.760
Here, let me give you
some examples.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:08.553
Come over here.

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:14.475
So I typed “make me a person 
with exactly five freckles”.

00:07:14.976 --> 00:07:16.602
So this one's from Dall-E 2.

00:07:16.602 --> 00:07:18.688
This one is from Stable Diffusion

00:07:18.688 --> 00:07:20.565
and this one is from Midjourney.

00:07:20.565 --> 00:07:22.483
So it's like, you know, 
great job.

00:07:22.483 --> 00:07:25.570
You've got a red haired person.

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:27.196
They're more likely to have freckles.

00:07:27.196 --> 00:07:30.116
But there are not exactly
five freckles here.

00:07:30.741 --> 00:07:34.328
Here that doesn't really matter
because we see a freckly face.

00:07:34.662 --> 00:07:37.331
But hands require higher standards.

00:07:37.748 --> 00:07:39.709
Look at our apple-holding man again.

00:07:39.709 --> 00:07:41.377
I made 3 other variations.

00:07:41.502 --> 00:07:44.172
The hands are all weird, but 
don't look at them right now.

00:07:44.338 --> 00:07:48.134
It changed the shirt stripes, 
the buttons, the apple style...

00:07:48.593 --> 00:07:51.262
None of that matters because 
it's stripe-like

00:07:51.262 --> 00:07:54.015
button-like and apple-like.

00:07:54.223 --> 00:07:56.350
But hand-like isn't good enough.

00:07:57.602 --> 00:07:59.812
I came away from this thinking 
a couple of things.

00:07:59.812 --> 00:08:02.231
AI art is basically 
bad at art.

00:08:02.231 --> 00:08:04.275
We're just able to see it with hands...

00:08:04.525 --> 00:08:06.944
and B, it's never going 
to get any better.

00:08:07.153 --> 00:08:09.071
But both of those things 
are a bit wrong.

00:08:09.071 --> 00:08:12.158
I will say that the newest 
AI art generator to come out

00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:15.286
at the time of this video is
Midjourney version 5

00:08:15.286 --> 00:08:18.164
and they made some progress 
with hands for sure...

00:08:18.706 --> 00:08:21.667
but it's not totally fixed yet.

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:24.045
Don't tell the AI

00:08:24.045 --> 00:08:25.087
to hold an umbrella.

00:08:29.258 --> 00:08:31.177
I think they're spending lots of time

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:33.888
on some things that you appreciate,
which is why you like the images

00:08:33.888 --> 00:08:36.015
and a lot of stuff that you 
don't actually even notice.

00:08:36.015 --> 00:08:38.351
I think that for a lot of natural scenery
or something like that

00:08:38.351 --> 00:08:40.436
I feel like model might be better
at that than people.

00:08:40.436 --> 00:08:41.938
And they are working on two things.

00:08:41.938 --> 00:08:44.899
First, they have the AI look at 
a ton more pictures

00:08:44.899 --> 00:08:46.943
which requires more computing power.

00:08:46.943 --> 00:08:48.986
They're trying to solve that 
on a big scale

00:08:48.986 --> 00:08:51.405
because if you want to train

00:08:51.405 --> 00:08:53.866
on more than a handful of images...

00:08:53.866 --> 00:08:56.452
if you want to train 
more than 100 images

00:08:56.452 --> 00:08:58.412
this would take tremendous resources
from you

00:08:58.412 --> 00:09:00.289
to retrain the model itself.

00:09:00.289 --> 00:09:03.459
The other solution might be 
to invite more people...

00:09:03.876 --> 00:09:05.086
into the museum.

00:09:05.086 --> 00:09:06.254
There's an interesting analog.

00:09:06.254 --> 00:09:08.297
So like, have you heard of 
like ChatGPT?

00:09:08.297 --> 00:09:11.592
The big difference was that it 
basically used human feedback.

00:09:11.592 --> 00:09:13.511
So like they generated 
many, many sentences

00:09:13.511 --> 00:09:16.222
and asked people to rate which ones are good
and which ones are not good.

00:09:16.222 --> 00:09:17.431
They basically fine tune the model

00:09:17.431 --> 00:09:18.641
so that it would generate sentences

00:09:18.641 --> 00:09:20.059
that are convincing to people.

00:09:20.059 --> 00:09:21.727
I guess it would require 
a lot of engineering

00:09:21.727 --> 00:09:23.896
to get people to label so much data.

00:09:23.896 --> 00:09:26.190
But I think if we could just 
get like people to rank...

00:09:26.566 --> 00:09:29.193
how good the images are 
generated by these models

00:09:29.193 --> 00:09:31.571
then like a lot of these issues 
will go away, actually.

00:09:31.612 --> 00:09:34.448
Because they're just training the models
to do what people like.

00:09:36.701 --> 00:09:39.245
It's not just the hand... 
teeth and abs.

00:09:39.245 --> 00:09:40.746
Anything where there's like a pattern...

00:09:40.746 --> 00:09:42.039
a large amount of something.

00:09:42.039 --> 00:09:45.376
It doesn't know the rule of
“there are this many”

00:09:45.376 --> 00:09:47.461
because it's trained on different amounts.

